the interview involves and also  some tips on how to do well in that  technical interview  sure so how just how lucy mentioned  before  you have to do your homework so prepare  and know  how the technical interview looks in  every different company  you can find it on glassdoor you can  probably find it on reddit you can find  it definitely on youtube  just make sure that you know which type  it is the interviews in big tech  like google microsoft amazon apple they  are usually based  on lead code questions so to prepare for  this type of interview  practice data structures and  algorithmics and what you can do is  lead code catawares there's a lot of  websites when you can practice  and you get feedback straight away  because your code is run through some  tests  um there's a book called cracking the  coding interview  just read this whole book and do all the  problems in it and you're gonna get a  job  at big tech no problem but then there is  a new trend emerging  and that's something i like much more  which is the interviews are more  real life problems so that's how for  example my interview with canva looked  like i actually got a homework  which is creating some kind of app they  provided me a fake server  and then i had to create a couple of  features and because i was being hired  as an android engineer  i had to use all of the technologies and  frameworks  they were using and requiring at canva  so  i think now we should pass to lucy  because she has much more experience in  the more  soft skills related areas yeah so i can  talk a bit about the  behavioral side of things especially  because i've done tons and tons of  interviews  so i think the most important thing  before the interview is to really be  well prepared  um with maybe the sort of questions that  they might ask as carol mentioned  there's different websites that  sort of show you um what the classical  questions might be for a particular  company  and even if those questions don't end up  coming up in the interview  it would still help to prepare for them  so that if something similar comes along  you will be able to draw an experience  from an interview example that you  prepared earlier so  it always helps to be a bit more  prepared and to  try and um figure out what your skills  are  and learning how to communicate that to  the interviewer  and i think you know half an hour before  the interview is quite important  as well because you want to enter that  mind state where you're confident  and you want to show you know the best  side of you to the interviewer  so 30 minutes before an interview i  normally do my  power poses i would demonstrate how to  do power poses  anyways one more thing to keep in mind  is that the interviewer will always look  at your questions holistically  and the impression that you give it's  not really about the individual  questions  so if you you know stuff up one question  and didn't really answer it to the best  of your abilities just  um don't let that one question kind of  stop you from  doing well in the rest of your  interviews and i'll say the same thing  for technical interviews right yeah  totally  uh just don't really worry about it  because when you do the technical  interviews  you are not expected to get everything  hundred percent right  it's more of your thinking process  that's being  uh i don't want to say judged what's a  good word for that oh  assessed yeah yes yes so that's your  ability to  think logically to solve problems that's  what's being assessed  so also don't don't worry about it  because if your process  of getting the answers right even if you  didn't the answer  fast enough or perfect enough or in the  best  time complexity don't worry about it you  can still get the job so don't get  discouraged just keep up keep going  through all the other questions  nice yeah i think we've covered some  really important points and maybe just  to wrap up  you know um when you've reached that  final interview stage you went through  the whole interview  um what would you recommend doing to  like follow up with the interviewer  because i think normally i would send  like you know thank you email to  thank them for their time is that  something that you recommend doing as  well  i have never done that myself okay  i was always waiting uh for the answer  what i would say  is maybe if they don't contact you  follow up if they contact you with  rejection  also remember to follow up ask for  feedback ask for what  what could i improve what would make me  one percent better  and yeah that's something because also  remember  if you apply to a place once to to a  company once and they reject you it  doesn't mean  that you can never apply again you can  apply again usually in half a year  and then if they see the whole process  because they have files on you  uh if they see the whole process of you  learning and trying to improve that's  already  a huge advantage yeah that's the point i  completely forgot about because i just  thought about you know thanking the  interviewer or the recruiter for their  time but it's really important as well  to follow up to  you know if you don't get in just ask  for feedback and try to be proactive and  figure out what  you went wrong in so that in the future  when you apply again  you can improve on that and increase  your chances so  this brings us to the end of this video  let us know if you want to see more  videos like this and don't forget to  check out the video we have on carol's  channel  talking about the difference between  computer science and information systems  that's it for now please remember to  like and subscribe and we'll see you  next time  bye for now
 

